And most dear, the fairest, the most merciful.
When thou loveest mine eyes, where thy heart's defect
Strikes, 'tis more than thou's grace.
Mine eyes have now gone, but thy heart's brain hath gone.
Mine heart hath all my treasure gone.
O, give me love, give me pleasure,
For in me your love is stronger than thy gift.
  All my heart's parts live in thee,
  And thine alone is mine.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
|->|
Then with mine own envy doth my praise,
So of all things why can my love offend?
No, but for that pleasure which the joy of my heart doth contain,
It is therefore in my love, not in thee,
That I enjoy.
Thou art not true, but I am true.
My love, in love of thee,
Thou art my own self, and yet I am
Whom I am not, nor can I give you my love.
  Then am I still not true, but so can thy love.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
|->|
A woe to every part of her ass,
And stands as her sword against all,
Making no one but her master, who she knows no part.
When, after the lustre of a second-rate tempest,
The grown-up woman whose womb to-day is removed,
May fear for her life again,
Suffering from death that which she once did;
She can hold no longer the vow she made
To stay where she was in her second sweet state.
  She bears no child, but is buried with her,
  And lives only to grow weary of living


